  "brief": "# Shared donors between 2020 Democratic primary candidates\n\n## The story I want to tell\n\nEarly in the 2020 Democratic primary, which candidates were drawing on the same people for\nmoney? For every pair of candidates, I want to know how many individual donors gave to both\nof them. The pairs at the top are the candidates competing for one pool of supporters.\n\n## The output I need\n\nOne row per pair of candidates who share at least one individual donor.\n\n| column | type | meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `candidate_x` | str | the alphabetically first candidate of the pair |\n| `candidate_y` | str | the alphabetically second candidate of the pair |\n| `count` | int | how many individual donors gave to both candidates |\n\nEach unordered pair appears once, with the two names in alphabetical order.\n\n## Sort order\n\n`count` descending. Where pairs tie, `candidate_x` ascending, then `candidate_y` ascending.\n\n## Files\n\n- `contributions.csv` - every itemized receipt reported on the 15 committees' April 2019\n  quarterly FEC filings, one row per receipt, 188,107 rows. It is the complete Schedule A of\n  each filing, not a subset. Columns include `filing_id`, `filer_committee_id_number`,\n  `transaction_id`, `entity_type`, `memo_code`, `contribution_date`,\n  `contribution_amount`, `contribution_aggregate`, and the contributor's name, address,\n  city, state, zip, employer and occupation as separate columns.\n- `candidates.csv` - the 15 candidates, each with `Candidate Name`, `Candidate Sex`,\n  `Committee Name` and `committee_id`.\n- `filings.csv` - one row per filing: `Candidate Name`, `committee_id`, `filing_id`,\n  `report_title`, `date_coverage_from`, `date_coverage_to`, `amended`.\n\nSource: Federal Election Commission electronic filings, April 2019 quarterly reports.",
