  "tolerance": 0.02,
  "curation_notes": [
    "DEVIATION: the source's inputs are 15 raw .fec filings, which carbonpaper cannot read - the csv reader takes no separator/header parameters, and .fec is ASCII-28 delimited with several record types of different widths and no header row. The filings were pre-converted to contributions.csv outside carbonpaper, so this case tests the ANALYSIS and not the ingestion. That matters because the subtlest trap in this data lives in the ingestion: FEC counts partnerships and LLCs (entity_type ORG) inside 'individuals itemized', and filtering to entity_type == IND alone undercounts.",
    "The conversion was verified independently of the notebook: summing SA17A receipts with entity_type in {IND, ORG} and no memo code reproduces each filer's own declared col_a_individuals_itemized total, for all 15 filings, exact to the cent.",
    "The golden's tie order is arbitrary - it is pandas groupby output order, and 56 of the 82 rows sit in a tie block. Curation re-sorts the tie blocks by candidate_x then candidate_y and the brief states that total order. Row CONTENT is untouched; only rows the source left in an undetermined order were moved.",
    "tolerance is 0.02, looser than the other cases. The counts depend on how donor identity is constructed from contributor name and address fields, which the brief deliberately does not specify - it is the analytical heart of the case. With 0.02 only counts above 50 get any slack at all (170 gets +/-3.4); everything below is effectively exact.",
    "Sorting by the same figure the tolerance applies to means a count that shifts can also change rank, so a small identity-rule difference shows up as row differences rather than cell differences. That is the honest reading: the ranking IS the answer."
  ]
